conv.courtesyPrecedent:
Decision Q5 in template-library-design.md covers the Tabular Editor 'full make-over' KPI card. tabulareditor.com's terms claim copyright on all site material and bar commercial reuse ('consultancy work'), and Tim is a consultant publishing under his own name.

Tim chose to build it anyway, but from scratch:
- his own Vega-Lite spec and his own invented sample data (he did not reuse even the daily-gap numbers reconstructed from their embed);
- none of their images or embed code;
- a substitute font (Segoe UI for Albert Sans, since Deneb cannot load web fonts);
- a credit to the author and the publisher ('after Ruben Van de Voorde (Tabular Editor)') with links, in the author field, the description, the README and the credits table;
- a README statement that it is an independent reimplementation and that the article and its images stay Tabular Editor ApS copyright;
- a list of every deviation.

He also had Claude draft a courtesy note (Vault\Projects\Deneb\tabular-editor-courtesy-note.md, status 'draft, for Tim to send', to the author or sales@tabulareditor.com). The note is upfront about how the card was made and offers to change the wording or take it down.

Rejected options:
- Asking permission first and holding the card back (could stall the work).
- An 'inspired by' card that is visibly different (dodges the ask).
The design doc frames design ideas as generally less protected than code or images, notes that from-scratch reimplementation is Deneb community norm (as with the Marsh-Patrick/Mundigl bullet), and says 'This is not legal advice.'

Related precedent (Q14, Duggan): a recreated report page keeps the original layout but uses BI Nexus colours and theme, and replaces the original's logo (the Northwind mark) with the BI Nexus logo. Sample numbers are reconstructed to match on-screen values, and every nudge is documented.

Lumeric Visuals appears to sell custom visuals. If so, a free MIT copy is a sharper case than a blog article, so this precedent points to checking their terms, a from-scratch spec, a visible credit, a courtesy note, and no copied branding.
